-- The learned widths of the ride dashboard's CURRENT and POWER dials (G §9.2),
-- one row per vehicle that has been ridden.
--
-- **A table of its own rather than two columns on VehicleRow, and that is the
-- whole point of `8.sqm`.** As columns they were reachable as `Vehicle` fields,
-- so `copy(gaugePeakCurrentA = ...)` plus `upsert` compiled and silently did
-- nothing: `upsert` had to COALESCE them back out of the row it was replacing,
-- because every caller of it holds a Vehicle SNAPSHOT and a snapshot can be
-- older than the last peak write. The rule was real; the interface taught its
-- opposite, and the only signal was a KDoc one layer away. Off the vehicle, the
-- unwritable setter stops existing rather than being documented.
--
-- **ABSENCE IS ZERO, and there is no third state.** A vehicle nobody has ridden
-- has no row here, and reads back as 0 A / 0 W — the same answer as a vehicle
-- that genuinely peaks at nothing, and both correctly open the dial on
-- GaugeScale.CURRENT_RUNGS_A's first rung. So `upsert` on a vehicle writes NO
-- row here, and the only statement that creates one is `upsert` below, called
-- from VehicleRepository.updateGaugePeaks.
--
-- REAL, because both are physical magnitudes the decoders already carry as
-- floats, and quantising them to the rung ladder here would throw away the
-- distinction the ladder itself needs (a peak that grows WITHIN a rung must be
-- retained, or it could never cross into the next one).
--
-- The declared cascade is documentation of intent only — SQLite leaves
-- PRAGMA foreign_keys OFF by default and neither driver factory turns it on
-- (AndroidSqliteDriver / JdbcSqliteDriver, both stock), so
-- SqlDelightVehicleRepository.delete deletes this row explicitly in the same
-- transaction as the vehicle. Same convention as PackRow, ControllerRow and
-- AlertLevelRow; this table adds no fourth one.
CREATE TABLE GaugePeakRow (
    vehicleId  TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
    currentA   REAL NOT NULL,
    powerW     REAL NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (vehicleId) REFERENCES VehicleRow(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

selectAll:
SELECT * FROM GaugePeakRow;

-- THE ONLY WRITER. INSERT OR REPLACE because the first peak a vehicle ever
-- learns has no row to update, and every later one has exactly one.
--
-- Unconstrained as to VALUE on purpose: it is also how the composer CLEARS the
-- peaks when a vehicle's controller set changes (G §9.2 item 7), which is the
-- one event that legitimately lowers them.
--
-- Constrained as to EXISTENCE, and that is not belt-and-braces. This replaced an
-- `UPDATE ... WHERE id`, which was a no-op for an id nobody stored; a plain
-- INSERT here would instead leave an orphan row, and RideDashboardComponent can
-- reach that. Its guard is non-guest and non-demo -- NOT persisted -- and its
-- vehicle comes from `activeVehicle`, which nothing disconnects when a vehicle is
-- deleted (SettingsComponent just calls `delete`). So a rider connected to a
-- vehicle they delete from Settings keeps a live dashboard whose next rung
-- crossing would write a parentless row, AFTER the delete that could have cleaned
-- it up. Vehicle ids are caller-supplied strings, so that row would be adopted by
-- whoever next reuses the id -- the same argument `deleteByVehicle`'s WHERE
-- clause exists for.
upsert:
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO GaugePeakRow(vehicleId, currentA, powerW)
SELECT :vehicleId, :currentA, :powerW WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM VehicleRow WHERE id = :vehicleId);

deleteByVehicle:
DELETE FROM GaugePeakRow WHERE vehicleId = :vehicleId;
